i\partial_t u + \partial_x^2 u + \lambda t^{\alpha_1} t^{\alpha_2} |x|^{\beta_1} x^{\beta_2}
|u|^2 u = 0, \quad (t,x) \in \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}, \\
u(0,x) = u_0(x),
\end{cases}$$ with the parameters satisfying $\alpha_i, \beta_i \in \{0,1\}$, $\alpha_1 + \beta_1 > -1$, $\alpha_2 + \beta_2 < 0$, the third condition, the scattering properties of the focusing case are studied. We prove scattering occurs when the $L^2$ norm of the initial data is sufficiently small, aside from a finite number of exceptional cases. Furthermore, the long-term behavior of solutions is quantified in terms of the parameters $\alpha_i, \beta_i$. We also calculate the precise asymptotic decay rate of solutions in both space and time for all cases, up to a possible logarithmic factor.

